// Regression coverage for #161: a static-factory receiver
// (Class<T>::variant(args), where T is inferable from a value argument)
// chained directly into a method call with no intermediate `const`
// annotation used to resolve to the wrong/unresolved specialization and
// ICE — for ANY method, not just own-type-param ones (isSome/unwrap here
// are plain, non-generic-returning methods; #158 covers the
// chained-own-type-param-method case separately). Root cause was
// namespace-wrapped generic classes (every stdlib generic class,
// including Option<T>, lives in a `namespace` block) never getting
// registered in tinox-typecheck's call-site type-argument-unification
// table at all — only top-level (non-namespaced) generic classes were.
// (Option<Int64>::none() — a zero-arg factory where T isn't inferable
// from any argument at all — is a DIFFERENT, still-open limitation,
// #166, not covered by this fix or this test.)
// expect: true
// expect: 1
import tinox.core.option;

class Main
{
    fnc main() -> Int32 {
        println(Option<Int64>::some(1).isSome());
        println(Option<Int64>::some(1).unwrap());
        return 0;
    }
}
